 ;; Measured 2026-09-01, by doing exactly what the previous entry said would
 ;; verify it. The answer is no. Both recorded custody locations hold the SAME
 ;; seed, and it is not the key that signs the live head:
 ;;
 ;;   kagi kotobase-ipni-publisher (net-kotobase)  -> 12D3KooWGmq2x23J…Usz3
 ;;   keychain publisher-seed-v2                   -> 12D3KooWGmq2x23J…Usz3  (same pubkey)
 ;;   the key signing GET /ipni/v1/head            -> 12D3KooWGxKwrwhq…Bt7X
 ;;
 ;; The held seed derives the THIRD entry in :identity-history -- the identity
 ;; this file named until the 2026-08-31 audit. The rotation that produced the
 ;; current identity never wrote its seed to either location.
 ;;
 ;; Where the current key actually is: `IPNI_ED25519_SEED_B64URL`, a Cloudflare
 ;; Worker secret on `net-kotobase-private-ipni-drain`. Worker secrets are
 ;; write-only, so the identity that publishes the corpus CANNOT be signed for
 ;; by any operator holding only these two vaults. That is not a lost key; it
 ;; is a key with exactly one holder, and the holder is a deploy target.
 ;;
 ;; :custody-verified below says :match, and it was true of the key it tested
 ;; on 2026-08-27. It is not a statement about the key publishing now.
 :ipni.publisher/current-custody
 {:status :mismatch
  :as-of "2026-09-01"
  :held-identity "12D3KooWGmq2x23J59LmqyBT5ik8pK9gEz7N3Y4vdJidezf7Usz3"
  :head-signing-identity "12D3KooWGxKwrwhqzVDKynC6PzP3vSWC5F3JjjmaqWyTE91YBt7X"
  :how "took each item once by its known identifier (never enumerated), derived the Ed25519 public key from the seed, built the identity multihash and base58btc peer-id, and compared against the pubkey read off GET /ipni/v1/head"
  :current-key-location "cloudflare worker secret IPNI_ED25519_SEED_B64URL on net-kotobase-private-ipni-drain (write-only)"
  :consequence "an operator can publish only as the held identity; the corpus chain can be extended only by the drain worker"}

 ;; Re-measured 2026-09-08, by the advertise tool itself rather than by hand:
 ;; it derives the public identity from whatever the vault returns and prints
 ;; it before writing anything. The answer had moved AGAIN, and not to the
 ;; identity recorded directly above:
 ;;
 ;;   kagi kotobase-ipni-publisher (net-kotobase) -> 12D3KooWDscME2qEggsQ…ay2of
 ;;   :current-custody :held-identity says         -> 12D3KooWGmq2x23J59Lm…Usz3
 ;;   the key signing GET /ipni/v1/head            -> 12D3KooWGxKwrwhqzVDK…Bt7X
 ;;
 ;; Three identities, three records, one vault. The derived key is the FIRST
 ;; entry in :identity-history -- the one this file marks
 ;; `:custody :unrecoverable`, which is now false of it and was true of
 ;; nothing measurable at the time it was written.
 ;;
 ;; What this is NOT: a claim that someone rotated the vault. Nobody measured
 ;; between 2026-09-01 and today, so the window is a week wide and the cause is
 ;; unknown. Recording it as unknown, because a guess here becomes the next
 ;; person's premise -- which is exactly how :custody :unrecoverable got
 ;; written about a key that opens.
 ;;
 ;; Why it did not block publishing: the tool refuses to publish under an
 ;; identity this manifest does not record, and this one is recorded. What it
 ;; DID block was the first attempt, which passed the Usz3 chain tip as
 ;; --previous. PreviousID is per-publisher; chaining DscME2q's advertisement
 ;; onto Usz3's would have orphaned it. The tip was read off the wire instead:
 ;;   GET https://cid.contact/providers/12D3KooWDscME2qEggsQ… -> LastAdvertisement
 :ipni.publisher/custody-remeasured
 {:at "2026-09-08"
  :status :moved-again
  :derived-identity "12D3KooWDscME2qEggsQYHg6d5d1Ms3Vq9duK6U15yQ64ksay2of"
  :previously-recorded-as-held "12D3KooWGmq2x23J59LmqyBT5ik8pK9gEz7N3Y4vdJidezf7Usz3"
  :head-signing-identity "12D3KooWGxKwrwhqzVDKynC6PzP3vSWC5F3JjjmaqWyTE91YBt7X"
  :how "scripts/ipni-advertise.cljs took the item once by its known identifier, derived the Ed25519 public key, built the identity multihash and base58btc peer-id, and printed it. The seed was never displayed."
  :contradicts [:ipni.publisher/current-custody
                "the :custody :unrecoverable on the first :identity-history entry"]
  :cause :unknown
  :consequence "the two custody records in this file disagree with each other and with the wire. Neither may be cited without re-deriving."}

 ;; Decision 1 of ADR-2608160300: ONE key for the workspace, not one per
 ;; surface. Twelve keys is a key set managed by enumeration whether anyone
 ;; admits it or not, and the safety floor is explicit that credentials are
 ;; taken one at a time by known identifier. Same reasoning as the appview
 ;; catalog's one-name-not-four-hundred.
 :ipni.publisher/scope :one-key-for-the-workspace

 ;; Decision 2: IPNI is a PUBLISHING DISCIPLINE for every surface that puts a
 ;; CID into the world, not a feature of pinning. Measured 2026-08-26: 435 of
 ;; 510 kotoba.app.edn manifests carry a bundle CID, and none of them is a
 ;; pin -- they are archive objects. Making IPNI a pinning feature would put
 ;; the largest addressed corpus in the workspace permanently out of reach of
 ;; the discovery plane.
 :ipni.publisher/discipline :every-surface-that-publishes-cids

 ;; Where the indexer GETs the advertisement chain. Anonymous by construction:
 ;; cid.contact crawls without credentials, so a publisher behind auth can
 ;; never be read (pinning.kotobase.net answers 401 and always will).
 :ipni.publisher/publisher-origin "https://ipni.kotobase.net"

 ;; Where the bytes are. `kotobase.net` became advertisable on 2026-08-26:
 ;; the path gateway now answers a block request with the block, and the
 ;; bytes it returns were fetched and re-hashed to the CID that was asked
 ;; for. A subdomain gateway is for website origin isolation, not for block
 ;; retrieval, so ipfs.kotobase.net being broken does not block this.
 ;; ⚠ Re-measured 2026-08-31 and the two rows had swapped. The entry that
 ;; said :ready 404s the objects the live chain announces, and the entry that
 ;; said :broken serves them and re-hashes correctly. The 2026-08-26
 ;; measurement is not disputed -- it verified two OTHER objects -- but a
 ;; status recorded once is a claim about that day, and the advertisements
 ;; kept moving. This is exactly the hazard :ipni.publisher/blockers states
 ;; below in its own words: an advertisement naming an address that cannot
 ;; serve a verifiable block is worse than no advertisement.
