 ;; Which cid.contact surface answers freshly. This is not trivia: the first
 ;; three minutes of watching the provide advertisement land were spent
 ;; reading a CloudFront response cached BEFORE the announce, which reports
 ;; "not indexed" in exactly the same shape as "indexed and absent".
 ;;
 ;;   /cid/{cid}                     CloudFront. age 554-573s observed.
 ;;                                  A query-string nonce does NOT bust it.
 ;;   /routing/v1/providers/{cid}    cache-control max-age=7200.
 ;;   /multihash/{base58 multihash}  no age header; reflected a new
 ;;                                  advertisement within 15s.
 ;;
 ;; So :round-trip's description of /cid/{cid} as an uncached lookup surface
 ;; is wrong. Measure liveness on /multihash/, and read `age:` before
 ;; believing any of them.
 :ipni.publisher/lookup-surfaces
 {:measured-at "2026-09-01T18:36:30Z"
  ;; Re-measured 2026-09-02. The earlier entry called /multihash/ :fresh on the
  ;; strength of one response that carried no age header -- but that was a MISS,
  ;; taken seconds after an announce. Asked again cold it is
  ;; `x-cache: Hit from cloudfront, age: 234`, and a query-string nonce does not
  ;; bust it (age went 213 -> 234 across two requests with different nonces, so
  ;; the query string is not in the cache key). All three surfaces are cached.
  ;;
  ;; What survives is the ORDER, which is what the reads actually rely on:
  ;; /multihash/ reflected a new advertisement in 15s while /cid/ and
  ;; /routing/v1/ were still serving `age: 1518` against `max-age=7200`.
  ;; It is the shortest-lived surface, not an uncached one.
  :least-stale ["https://cid.contact/multihash/{base58btc-multihash}"]
  :cached ["https://cid.contact/cid/{cid}"
           "https://cid.contact/routing/v1/providers/{cid}"
           "https://cid.contact/multihash/{base58btc-multihash}"]
  :rule "read the age header; a cached miss and a real absence are the same bytes"
  :consequence "a PRESENCE read through cache is still a presence someone published. An ABSENCE is not safe to read this way, which is why the removal check carries two controls rather than trusting one 200."}

